Yes. The architecture is viable, but there are **different levels of “provable.”** You do not need a TEE for WorkerKit V1. You need one only when you want to make the stronger claim:

> “This exact WorkerKit runtime, with this exact agent configuration and policy, actually produced this run without the operator secretly modifying the environment.”

That distinction matters.

The standards landscape actually fits WorkerKit extremely well.

## WorkerKit should treat every run like a software supply-chain build

Instead of:

```text
agent starts
→ does stuff
→ creates answer
→ submits
```

make the canonical object:

```text
WorkerRun
│
├── WorkerManifest
│   ├── workerkit version
│   ├── agent config hash
│   ├── skill/process versions
│   ├── source repo + commit
│   ├── model routing policy
│   ├── tool permissions
│   ├── verifier policy
│   └── execution environment
│
├── Inputs
│   └── content-addressed hashes
│
├── RunEvents
│   ├── model calls
│   ├── tool calls
│   ├── costs
│   ├── timings
│   ├── decisions
│   ├── intermediate artifacts
│   └── retries/repairs
│
├── Outputs
│   └── content-addressed hashes
│
├── Verification
│   ├── tests
│   ├── critic/verifier results
│   └── submission checks
│
└── Outcome
    ├── submitted
    ├── accepted/rejected
    ├── reward
    └── marketplace receipt
```

Then cryptographically bind that entire thing together.

There are already standards intended for almost exactly this. The current in-toto Attestation Framework v1.2 describes authenticated metadata bound to artifact digests, with signed envelopes, and supports arbitrary predicate types. ([GitHub][1])

Even better, in-toto already has a **Runtime Trace predicate** specifically intended to capture process execution, network activity and file accesses for an operation. ([GitHub][2])

That means WorkerKit doesn't need to invent its cryptographic vocabulary from scratch.

