# What a TEE still DOES NOT prove

This is critical.

Suppose the attested worker calls:

```text
OpenAI
Anthropic
OpenRouter
Reddit
Gigs.sh
```

The TEE can prove:

```text
WorkerKit made this request
WorkerKit received these bytes
```

depending on how you capture them.

It cannot magically prove:

```text
OpenAI actually ran model X internally
```

unless OpenAI signs that claim.

Likewise:

```text
provider charged $0.0142
```

should ideally come from a provider-signed receipt or authenticated API evidence.

Therefore evidence quality should be explicit:

```text
SELF_REPORTED
SIGNED_BY_WORKER
TEE_ATTESTED
TLS_NOTARIZED
PROVIDER_SIGNED
INDEPENDENTLY_VERIFIED
ONCHAIN_SETTLED
```

Never turn them all into:

```text
VERIFIED=true
```

That would destroy the whole point.

---

# And zk proofs?

I would **not try to zk-prove the whole agent**.

A zkVM is useful when you have:

```text
program
+
committed deterministic inputs
=
deterministic output
```

Then:

```text
proof(program executed correctly)
```

is meaningful.

An autonomous worker involves:

```text
LLM generation
web APIs
network
tool calls
timing
external state
randomness
marketplaces
```

A zk proof can't tell you whether an external fact was true unless that fact enters through some trusted/verified oracle mechanism.

So doing:

```text
Hermes
→ browser
→ models
→ Reddit
→ GitHub
→ APIs
→ 40 minute task
```

inside a zkVM would be massively complicated and still wouldn't magically solve the external trust problem.

Use ZK selectively later:

```text
prove cost calculation correct
prove score calculation correct
prove payout > X without disclosing amount
prove reputation threshold
prove secret dataset satisfied condition
```

not:

```text
zk-prove GPT did research
```

